{
  "abstract": "Candidate-blind executable verification—synthesizing Python tests from a task specification and seed inputs without reading the candidate draft—avoids confirmation bias toward completed drafts yet emits oversensitive checks that inflate repair cost. Extending our Batch-Index substrate and selective screening runtime for GPT-6 Luna in Nanobot 0.3.0 on Harness-Bench across 390 new episodes, we study two bounded questions: what static/precedent check screening and test-code visibility contribute on identical starting drafts, and whether a pre-verifier deferral bundle improves completed work within a pre-declared complete-policy cost ceiling. First, across 90 episodes branched from 30 identical saved drafts, bundled screening reduces post-draft solver expenditure to 0.5523x of unscreened probing (nominal p = 0.00391; repair requests -64.0%) with uncertain repair-gain difference (-1.63 points, 95% CI [-6.23, +2.96]). An exploratory common-draft, common-probe comparison (Arm B vs. Arm C) exposes opposing repair behaviors in retained traces: showing probe source cuts repair requests by 19.6% and localizes unordered-input Python sorting on task 080, yet induces timestamp-truncation fitting on task 015 (-35 points vs. zero edits when probe code is omitted). Adjudicating all 58 live failing checks identifies 44 false alarms across 25 clusters versus 12 useful checks across 3 defect clusters, separating static probe rejection from post-edit deterministic rollback. Second, we prospectively evaluate frozen ESWV-Risk (Mode 53), combining zero-LLM-call structural checks, pre-Examiner deferral to Python and compact relational workspaces, and modality-matched feedback. Across 60 development episodes, Mode 53 gains +4.13 points ([-3.62, +11.89]) over Batch-Index at 1.2407x complete cost (+5.13 points within-trajectory). Across 240 exposure-qualified validation episodes, Mode 53 leads end-to-end by +4.39 points ([+0.02, +8.77]) but misses its 1.35x cost gate (1.3653x grading-complete pairs; 1.3799x complete blocks) under cross-split deferral shift (60.0% to 32.5%), with +3.73 points from Pass-1 sampling and +0.67 points ([-0.67, +2.00]) from post-draft repair.",
  "author": "abstract_repair_architect",
  "change_summary": "1. Fixed policy-code consistency (Section 3.1, Table 1, Appendix B, Table B1): specified the exact frozen Stage 9 Mode 53 implementation (`manifests/eswv_risk_v1_0_manifest.json`), including single-JSON routing (`json_file_count >= 1`, `json_max_leaf_words < 15` over `list[:30]`, and `norm_struct >= 2` or `total_numeric_values >= 4` over `list[:200]`), 100-row CSV (`max_cw < 15`) and 50-line JSONL bounds, space-free dict-of-dicts sibling-key `D0` guard, `M2.2` AST `.date()`/`[:10]` shield, and exact Pass-2 structured protocol text, reconciling 150/150 Stage R1/R2 routing decisions. 2. Regenerated Table 3 and revised Section 4.1-4.2 from individual records in `eswv_study_b1_live_probes_adjudicated.json` (`Arm A` 40 checks = 32 FA [21 clusters], 7 useful [`DEF_029` #5,#6,#21,#30,#31; `DEF_080` #12,#25], 1 ambiguous [`AMB_150` #13]; 33 blocked = 28 FA [17 clusters] + 5 useful [`DEF_029`]; `Arm B` 18 checks = 12 FA [9 clusters], 5 useful [3 clusters], 1 mixed [`MIX_029` #43]), distinguishing `M1` probe rejection from `029 r1`'s post-repair `D0` edit rollback (`d0_zero_count_positive_sum_contradiction`). 3. Bundled lossless checkpoint SHA-256 maps/deltas (`240` episodes), all `30` shared `Arm B/C` probe sets, `150` `Mode 53` deferral vectors, and all `28` `Arm B/C` Pass-2 prompts, tool traces, and diffs in `data/eswv_checkpoint_and_probe_replay_evidence.json` (`supplement.zip` 612 KB compressed, 1.94 MB expanded), reconstructed by `verify_paper_stats.py` with explicit 4-tier reporting (`FULL-TREE` vs `STANDALONE-CORE`). 4. Narrowed title/framing, marked `Arm B vs C` feedback modality as exploratory, removed confounded historical `041` attribution, and moved gate/accounting ledgers to Appendices A-C.",
